 The distance from the gate to the tree is {val_b} m. Which distance is {comp_word} in meters?\", \"reasoning\": \"F03 compares two distances, each given in meters, which matches the competency's distance in meters.\"}, \"measuring_tools\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F02\", \"template\": \"Read the measurement on the ruler carefully.\", \"reasoning\": \"F02 is a hint that directs the learner to a ruler; ruler is in student_vocab and in introduces_vocab, so the tool named is within the lc's vocabulary.\"}}, \"failing_families\": [\"F01\", \"F05\"], \"failing_templates\": [\"Compare {val_a} {count_noun} and {val_b} {count_noun2}.\", \"The {comparison} {measure} is {answer} {count_noun}.\"], \"overall\": \"FAIL\", \"reasoning\": \"F05 prints the measure slot from the two compare measures (distance or length) but its count_noun slot is derived from unit alone, not from task_type. Its members include centimeter (cm), so a distance can be printed in centimeters, which contradicts the competency's distance in meters. F05 also prints the count_noun member in singular form beside answer values that include 10, 12, 20 (answer printed_runs 1 to 12, 15, 18, 20 to 21), giving a plural number followed by a singular unit. F01 has the same singular-with-plural-number defect: its count_noun members are centimeter (cm) and meter (m) only, beside val_a printed_runs 1 to 10, 12, 20 and val_b printed_runs 1 to 12, 15, 18, 20 to 21. F01 is hint text, but the defect is learner-facing wording. F02, F03, F04, F06, F07, F08 and F09 raise no fault in the domains given: F03 prints distances only in m, F04 and F07 and F09 use cm only, F06 uses only the cumulative vocabulary and no NOT_YET_KNOWN term appears in any family. Limit: F03 does not state whether val_a and val_b can be equal, so an equal-value render (no single longer or shorter answer) is not ruled out by these domains.\"}",
